  "title": "More from Less: 3D Gaussian Splatting Digital Twins with Iterative Expansion and Trajectory Compensation for Sparse-Demonstration Bimanual Manipulation",
  "abstract_en": "Bimanual manipulation requires coordinated arm motion and object contact, but costly demonstrations cover only limited interaction states. We therefore propose MFL (More from Less), combining failure-region expansion in digital twins with deployment-time trajectory compensation for sparse-demonstration learning. A structured vision–language–action (VLA) policy first learns joint bimanual actions from real demonstrations using shared task context and training-time geometric supervision. To extend interaction experience, MFL decouples the background and manipulable objects into independent 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) submodels and combines them with separate collision geometry and external physical parameters. Policy rollouts in this digital twin locate failures, around which feasible initial states are sampled for candidate generation. Complete trajectories that finish the task and pass physics and safety checks enter real-dominant retraining. The updated policy then identifies subsequent expansion regions. At deployment, 2D target trajectories extracted from asynchronously generated videos are aligned with the current state and mapped to actions. Valid references are fused with policy outputs only at high risk. Both base and fused actions pass through a shared quadratic-programming filter and nonlinear recheck. Across six real-robot task categories, the full system achieves a four-stage macro-average success rate of 89.00%, exceeding structured VLA by 13.17 percentage points. In a separate step-recovery evaluation, the complete compensation configuration achieves 67.78% recovery.",
  "source_tex_sha256": "e68ba2bc567ca199bf9fb46d61dd3c09c0c590f0388379f8c468a1a24091b39a",
